<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tushpa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Van Fortress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tushpa was the capital of the kingdom of Urartu, built on a long limestone rock above the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. King Sarduri I fortified it around 840 BCE, and his successors carved their annals and tombs into the rock. The citadel, known today as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/van-fortress">Van Fortress</a>, remained in use under later empires, and a trilingual inscription of the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> is cut into its face.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tushpa.jpg" alt="Tushpa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandros.Papadopoulos from flickr.com · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_castle,_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
